\section{Overview}
This paper provides a step-by-step guide for analyzing multibody dynamics.
Its central goal is to show how the same physical system can be analyzed in multiple mathematical representations without changing the underlying physics.

The organizing idea is an affine split between \emph{drift} and \emph{control}:
\begin{equation}
\dot x = f_{\mathrm{drift}}(x) + G(x)u.
\end{equation}
Here drift is the state-determined part of dynamics, while control is the input-driven part.

Symmetry is already visible at this level.
Physical laws do not depend on coordinate labels or frame choices.
Changing coordinates changes equation appearance, not physical behavior.
Because of this representation independence, the same conclusions can be recovered from Euler--Lagrange, Newton--Euler, product-of-exponentials screw formulations, operational-space, or coordinate-free geometric forms.

We also use the zero torque counterfactual (ZTCF): at a fixed time point, keep the same instantaneous state and set actuation to zero.
This isolates baseline passive behavior from incremental control effect and makes the state contribution explicit.

\subsection{Layout}
Each analysis section has the same structure:
\begin{enumerate}
\item general equations in that formalism,
\item a concrete double-pendulum example,
\item a procedure checklist for implementation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{State Accumulation and Causality}
The present state stores past acceleration history.
In generalized coordinates,
\begin{equation}
\dot q(t)=\dot q(t_0)+\int_{t_0}^{t}\ddot q(\sigma)\,d\sigma,
\qquad
h_m(t)=M(q(t))\dot q(t),
\end{equation}
and in first-order state form,
\begin{equation}
x(t)=x(t_0)+\int_{t_0}^{t}\mathcal{F}(x(\sigma),u(\sigma))\,d\sigma.
\end{equation}
So high-speed behavior should be interpreted through accumulated state and not only through instantaneous input.

\section{ZTCF and Drift: Precise Interpretation}
\subsection{What Is Drift and What Is ZTCF?}
For control-affine dynamics
\begin{equation}
\dot x=f_{\mathrm{drift}}(x)+G(x)u,
\end{equation}
drift is a \emph{state-to-vector-field map} defined over the full state space.

Define the drift snapshot at time $t$ as
\begin{equation}
\dot x_{\mathrm{drift},t}\coloneqq f_{\mathrm{drift}}\bigl(x(t)\bigr).
\end{equation}

ZTCF is an \emph{evaluation rule at a single state}:
\begin{equation}
\dot x_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}\coloneqq\dot x\bigl(x(t),u=0\bigr).
\end{equation}
So ZTCF is not a different physical term; it is the drift value sampled at a specific state.
Numerically, in an unconstrained model with this decomposition,
\begin{equation}
\dot x_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}=\dot x_{\mathrm{drift},t}.
\end{equation}
Accordingly, ZTCF can be treated as drift with a time-indexed subscript.

For the manipulator form
\begin{equation}
M(q)\ddot q+h(q,\dot q)=\tau,
\end{equation}
we have
\begin{equation}
\ddot q(\tau)= -M^{-1}h + M^{-1}\tau,
\qquad
\ddot q_{\mathrm{drift},t}=\left[-M^{-1}h\right]_{(q(t),\dot q(t))},
\qquad
\ddot q_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}=\ddot q(0)=\ddot q_{\mathrm{drift},t}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Why ZTCF Is Time-Local}
ZTCF is a local counterfactual defined at a fixed timestep (equivalently, a fixed state).
It does \emph{not} imply that future zero-actuation motion is obtained by freezing drift.
The state evolves, and drift changes with it.

Local first-order propagation is
\begin{equation}
x(t+\Delta t)=x(t)+\Delta t\,\mathcal{F}(x(t),u(t))+\mathcal{O}(\Delta t^2),
\end{equation}
so ZTCF is the $u=0$ evaluation of this local vector field, not a global future trajectory identity.

\subsection{What Changes When Constraints Are Present?}
With constraints,
\begin{equation}
M\ddot q+h=\tau+J_c^T\lambda+\tau_{\mathrm{ext}},
\qquad
J_c\ddot q+\dot J_c\dot q=0,
\end{equation}
and the multiplier depends on both the state and the actuation channel.

Define $\lambda_0$ as the multiplier under $\tau=0$ at the same state.
Then
\begin{equation}
\ddot q_{\mathrm{drift},t}^{c}=\left[M^{-1}\bigl(-h+J_c^T\lambda_0+\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}\bigr)\right]_{(q(t),\dot q(t))},
\qquad
\ddot q_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}^{c}=\ddot q_{\mathrm{drift},t}^{c}.
\end{equation}
Thus, constrained ZTCF still represents the passive baseline, but now ``passive'' includes constraint reactions required by compatibility.

This refines rather than changes the core interpretation.
In constrained systems, ``passive'' means state, external loads, \emph{and} compatibility-induced reaction channels.

\subsection{Tangent-Hyperplane Interpretation}
At fixed state, the acceleration-level constraint
\begin{equation}
J_c\ddot q+\dot J_c\dot q=0
\end{equation}
defines an affine hyperplane of admissible accelerations in $\ddot q$-space.

ZTCF gives a specific base point on that hyperplane (the drift snapshot at time $t$).
Control then moves along allowable directions within that same affine set:
\begin{equation}
\ddot q = \ddot q_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}^{c} + B_c\bigl(x(t)\bigr)\,\tau,
\end{equation}
for some constraint-consistent input map $B_c(x)$.

The tangent-hyperplane view is therefore exact, not heuristic.
It is a precise local geometric interpretation: ZTCF is the anchor point, and control provides admissible tangent directions of motion from that anchor.

\subsection{Practical Takeaway}
Use ZTCF as an instantaneous diagnostic of passive or accumulated effects.
Use drift as the underlying field for simulation and analysis.
In constrained settings, include reaction channels in the passive baseline when interpreting ZTCF.

\subsection{Linearization and the ZTCF/Drift Connection}
\subsubsection*{What Linearization Does}
Linearization converts a nonlinear model into a first-order local approximation around an operating point.
For a general state model
\begin{equation}
\dot x=\mathcal{F}(x,u),
\end{equation}
choose a reference pair $(x_\star,u_\star)$ and define perturbations
\begin{equation}
\delta x\coloneqq x-x_\star,
\qquad
\delta u\coloneqq u-u_\star.
\end{equation}
Then the first-order expansion is
\begin{equation}
\delta\dot x\approx A_\star\,\delta x+B_\star\,\delta u,
\qquad
A_\star\coloneqq\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{F}}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_\star,u_\star)},
\qquad
B_\star\coloneqq\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{F}}{\partial u}\right|_{(x_\star,u_\star)}.
\end{equation}
Equivalently, in non-perturbation form,
\begin{equation}
\dot x\approx \dot x_\star + A_\star(x-x_\star)+B_\star(u-u_\star),
\qquad
\dot x_\star\coloneqq\mathcal{F}(x_\star,u_\star).
\end{equation}
So linearization does not replace the nonlinear physics globally; it provides a local small-signal model valid near the chosen operating point.

This local model is frequently used to:
\begin{itemize}
\item analyze local stability and modal structure through eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $A_\star$,
\item design feedback laws (e.g., LQR, pole placement, linear MPC),
\item build local estimators and observers (including EKF-style linearized propagation),
\item construct gain-scheduled controllers by re-linearizing along a trajectory.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Direct Relation to ZTCF and Drift}
For control-affine dynamics,
\begin{equation}
\dot x=f_{\mathrm{drift}}(x)+G(x)u,
\end{equation}
the ZTCF value at time $t$ is
\begin{equation}
\dot x_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}=f_{\mathrm{drift}}\bigl(x(t)\bigr).
\end{equation}
Linearizing at the ZTCF operating point $(x(t),u=0)$ gives
\begin{equation}
\dot x\approx \dot x_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}+A_t\bigl(x-x(t)\bigr)+B_tu,
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
A_t=\left.\frac{\partial f_{\mathrm{drift}}}{\partial x}\right|_{x(t)},
\qquad
B_t=G\bigl(x(t)\bigr).
\end{equation}
This makes the hierarchy explicit:
\begin{itemize}
\item ZTCF/drift provides the \emph{zeroth-order anchor} (baseline vector at the current state),
\item linearization adds \emph{first-order sensitivity} to state and input perturbations.
\end{itemize}

In constrained dynamics, the same logic applies on the admissible acceleration set.
At fixed state, $\mathcal{H}_t$ defines feasible accelerations, $\ddot q^{c}_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}$ is the passive anchor on $\mathcal{H}_t$, and the linearized constrained model describes local tangent perturbations around that anchor.

Therefore, connecting ZTCF/drift to linearization is not out of place.
It is a natural bridge from geometric interpretation (anchor plus admissible directions) to standard control and estimation workflows.

\section{Notation and Modeling Assumptions}
Generalized coordinates are $q\in\mathbb{R}^n$, with velocities $\dot q$ and accelerations $\ddot q$.
The dot notation denotes time derivatives.

Generalized effort $\tau$ denotes torques for rotational coordinates and forces for translational coordinates.
We use $A^{-1}$ for matrix inverse and $A^T$ for transpose.

For unconstrained rigid-body systems, $M(q)$ is symmetric positive definite.
For constrained systems, holonomic constraints are written as $\phi(q)=0$ with Jacobian $J_c(q)=\partial\phi/\partial q$.

All ZTCF comparisons are made at fixed instantaneous state unless explicitly noted.

\section{Running Example: Planar Double Pendulum}
We use a two-link planar pendulum throughout.
Let
\begin{equation}
q = \begin{bmatrix}\theta_1 \\ \theta_2\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\dot q = \begin{bmatrix}\dot\theta_1 \\ \dot\theta_2\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
with link lengths $(l_1,l_2)$ and masses $(m_1,m_2)$.

Endpoint kinematics:
\begin{equation}
x_2=l_1\cos\theta_1+l_2\cos(\theta_1+\theta_2),
\qquad
y_2=l_1\sin\theta_1+l_2\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2).
\end{equation}

Manipulator-form dynamics:
\begin{equation}
\tau = M(q)\ddot q + C(q,\dot q)\dot q + g(q).
\end{equation}
A standard model is
\begin{equation}
M(q)=
\begin{bmatrix}
(m_1+m_2)l_1^2 + m_2l_2^2 + 2m_2l_1l_2\cos\theta_2 & m_2l_2^2+m_2l_1l_2\cos\theta_2 \\
m_2l_2^2+m_2l_1l_2\cos\theta_2 & m_2l_2^2
\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
C(q,\dot q)\dot q=
\begin{bmatrix}
-2c\dot\theta_1\dot\theta_2-c\dot\theta_2^2 \\
c\dot\theta_1^2
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
c\coloneqq m_2l_1l_2\sin\theta_2,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
g(q)=
\begin{bmatrix}
(m_1+m_2)gl_1\cos\theta_1 + m_2gl_2\cos(\theta_1+\theta_2) \\
m_2gl_2\cos(\theta_1+\theta_2)
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
(Signs may change under different angle conventions, but the procedure is unchanged.)

\section{Euler--Lagrange Analysis Procedure}
\subsection{General Equations}
From $L(q,\dot q)=T(q,\dot q)-V(q)$,
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot q}\right)-\frac{\partial L}{\partial q}=\tau
\end{equation}
gives
\begin{equation}
\tau=M(q)\ddot q + C(q,\dot q)\dot q + g(q).
\end{equation}

Affine drift-control form:
\begin{equation}
\ddot q=\ddot q_{\mathrm{drift}}+\ddot q_{\mathrm{ctrl}},
\qquad
\ddot q_{\mathrm{drift}}=-M^{-1}(C\dot q+g),
\qquad
\ddot q_{\mathrm{ctrl}}=M^{-1}\tau.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Double-Pendulum Instance}
Use the $M,C,g$ from the running-example section directly.
At fixed state,
\begin{equation}
h(q,\dot q)\coloneqq C(q,\dot q)\dot q+g(q),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\ddot q_0=-M^{-1}h,
\qquad
\ddot q_u=M^{-1}(\tau-h),
\qquad
\ddot q_u-\ddot q_0=M^{-1}\tau.
\end{equation}
This gives the ZTCF attribution identity in generalized coordinates.

\subsection{Procedure Checklist}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Define coordinates and parameters.
\item Derive $T$ and $V$.
\item Compute $M,C,g$.
\item Evaluate drift term $-M^{-1}(C\dot q+g)$ at measured state.
\item Apply control and compute total acceleration.
\item Compare measured acceleration with model prediction.
\item Use residuals to diagnose unmodeled effects.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Constrained Euler--Lagrange Procedure}
\subsection{General Equations}
For holonomic constraints $\phi(q)=0$:
\begin{equation}
M\ddot q + C\dot q + g = \tau + J_c^T\lambda + \tau_{\mathrm{ext}},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
J_c(q)\ddot q+\dot J_c(q,\dot q)\dot q=0.
\end{equation}
At fixed state, this constraint defines an affine tangent hyperplane of admissible accelerations,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_t\coloneqq\left\{\ddot q\;\middle|\;J_c\ddot q=-\dot J_c\dot q\right\}.
\end{equation}
The constrained ZTCF acceleration is the passive anchor point on $\mathcal{H}_t$.

\subsection{Double-Pendulum Instance}
For example, constrain the endpoint to a circle:
\begin{equation}
\phi(q)=\bigl(x_2(q)-x_c\bigr)^2+\bigl(y_2(q)-y_c\bigr)^2-R^2=0.
\end{equation}
Then $J_c=\partial\phi/\partial q$, and $J_c^T\lambda$ yields the generalized reaction loads.

\subsection{Procedure Checklist}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Define $\phi(q)$ for geometry or contact.
\item Compute $J_c$ and $\dot J_c\dot q$.
\item Build the admissible hyperplane $\mathcal{H}_t$.
\item Solve the coupled system for $(\ddot q,\lambda)$.
\item Compute the passive anchor $\ddot q_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}^{c}$ on $\mathcal{H}_t$.
\item Recover generalized reaction loads $J_c^T\lambda$.
\item Compare constrained and unconstrained ZTCF baselines.
\item Interpret whether drift or constraints dominate behavior.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Newton--Euler Analysis Procedure}
\subsection{General Equations}
For each rigid body $i$,
\begin{equation}
m_i a_{c,i}=f_i^{\mathrm{in}}-f_i^{\mathrm{out}}+f_i^{\mathrm{ext}}+f_i^{\mathrm{ctrl}},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
I_i\dot\omega_i+\omega_i\times(I_i\omega_i)=n_i^{\mathrm{in}}-n_i^{\mathrm{out}}+n_i^{\mathrm{ext}}+n_i^{\mathrm{ctrl}}.
\end{equation}
Spatial form:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}(x)\dot v+\mathcal{C}(x,v)v+\mathcal{G}(x)=\mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{ctrl}}+\mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{constr}}+\mathcal{W}_{\mathrm{ext}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Double-Pendulum Instance}
Treat each link as a rigid body with its own center-of-mass balance.
Forward recursion gives $\omega_i,\dot\omega_i,a_{c,i}$.
Backward recursion gives joint forces and torques $(\tau_1,\tau_2)$.
This directly exposes internal load transfer between proximal and distal links.

\subsection{Procedure Checklist}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Define local frames, inertias, and COM offsets.
\item Run forward kinematics and acceleration recursion.
\item Apply external/contact loads at each body.
\item Run backward force-moment recursion.
\item Extract actuator torques and joint reactions.
\item Repeat with ZTCF to isolate passive load paths.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Product-of-Exponentials and Screw-Axis Procedure}
\subsection{General Equations}
The product-of-exponentials (PoE) model represents rigid-body kinematics on $SE(3)$ using fixed screw axes.
In space-frame form,
\begin{equation}
g_{sb}(q)=e^{\hat S_1 q_1}e^{\hat S_2 q_2}\cdots e^{\hat S_n q_n}g_{sb}(0),
\end{equation}
where $\hat S_i\in\mathfrak{se}(3)$ is the twist-hat representation of screw axis $S_i$.

Velocity and acceleration in screw coordinates satisfy
\begin{equation}
V_s = J_s(q)\dot q,
\qquad
\dot V_s = J_s(q)\ddot q + \dot J_s(q,\dot q)\dot q.
\end{equation}
The term $\dot J_s\dot q$ is the screw-coordinate analogue of velocity-product (Coriolis/centripetal) effects.

In spatial dynamics form, each body wrench balance is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal F_i = \mathcal I_i\dot{\mathcal V}_i + \operatorname{ad}_{\mathcal V_i}^{*}(\mathcal I_i\mathcal V_i) - \mathcal F_i^{\mathrm{ext}},
\end{equation}
and generalized actuation is recovered by projection through Jacobians.
This is dynamically equivalent to the Euler--Lagrange form and is often implemented by recursive Newton--Euler algorithms.

ZTCF is evaluated exactly as before: freeze state $(q(t),\dot q(t))$ and set actuation to zero.
Then
\begin{equation}
\ddot q_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}=\left[\ddot q(\tau=0)\right]_{(q(t),\dot q(t))},
\qquad
\dot V_{s,\mathrm{ZTCF},t}=J_s\ddot q_{\mathrm{ZTCF},t}+\dot J_s\dot q(t).
\end{equation}

\subsection{Double-Pendulum Instance}
For a planar two-revolute chain (space frame at joint 1), one convenient choice is
\begin{equation}
S_1=\begin{bmatrix}0&0&1&0&0&0\end{bmatrix}^T,
\qquad
S_2=\begin{bmatrix}0&0&1&0&-l_1&0\end{bmatrix}^T,
\end{equation}
with home transform $g_{sb}(0)$ from base to endpoint.
Then
\begin{equation}
g_{sb}(q)=e^{\hat S_1\theta_1}e^{\hat S_2\theta_2}g_{sb}(0),
\qquad
V_s = J_s(q)\begin{bmatrix}\dot\theta_1\\\dot\theta_2\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
This gives a coordinate-consistent route from joint rates to endpoint twists and wrenches.

\subsection{Procedure Checklist}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Define home configuration $g_{sb}(0)$ and screw axes $S_i$.
\item Build PoE forward kinematics and space/body Jacobians.
\item Compute twist and bias acceleration terms $J\dot q$ and $\dot J\dot q$.
\item Run recursive Newton--Euler/spatial dynamics for wrench balance.
\item Project wrenches to joint torques and compare with Euler--Lagrange terms.
\item Evaluate ZTCF by setting actuation to zero at fixed state.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Operational-Space Analysis Procedure}
\subsection{General Equations}
Define task coordinates $x=\psi(q)$ with Jacobian $J=\partial\psi/\partial q$:
\begin{equation}
\ddot x=J\ddot q+\dot J\dot q,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\Lambda(x)\ddot x+\mu(x,\dot x)+p(x)=F_{\mathrm{ctrl}}+F_{\mathrm{constr}}+F_{\mathrm{ext}}.
\end{equation}
If task-level constraints are active, the same tangent-hyperplane interpretation applies in $\ddot x$-space.
ZTCF provides the passive anchor, and control moves along admissible tangent directions.

\subsection{Double-Pendulum Instance}
For endpoint task $x=[x_2\;y_2]^T$:
\begin{equation}
J(q)=
\begin{bmatrix}
-l_1\sin\theta_1-l_2\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2) & -l_2\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2) \\
l_1\cos\theta_1+l_2\cos(\theta_1+\theta_2) & +l_2\cos(\theta_1+\theta_2)
\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\Lambda=(J M^{-1}J^T)^{-1}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Procedure Checklist}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Choose task variables (position, orientation, or both).
\item Derive $J$ and $\dot J\dot q$.
\item Compute $\Lambda,\mu,p$.
\item Specify desired task acceleration or force.
\item Map task commands to joint torques.
\item Evaluate drift baseline in task space using ZTCF.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Coordinate-Free Geometric Procedure}
\subsection{General Equations}
On configuration manifold $Q$,
\begin{equation}
\nabla_{\dot q}\dot q + \operatorname{grad}V(q) = \mathcal{G}(q)u + \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{ext}} + \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{constr}}.
\end{equation}
Control-affine state form on $TQ$:
\begin{equation}
\dot x=f_{\mathrm{drift}}(x)+\sum_{i=1}^{m}g_i(x)u_i.
\end{equation}
In this intrinsic setting, admissible directions at $q$ naturally live in $T_qQ$ (or in a constrained affine subset), so the tangent-hyperplane interpretation is built in rather than appended later.

\subsection{Double-Pendulum Instance}
For the double pendulum,
\begin{equation}
Q=S^1\times S^1,
\end{equation}
and the kinetic metric induced by $T$ has matrix representation $M(q)$ in local coordinates.
The potential $V(q)$ and control map $\mathcal{G}(q)$ are inherited from the same physical model as in the previous formalism sections.

\subsection{Procedure Checklist}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Define $Q$ and admissible states on $TQ$.
\item Build the kinetic metric (from kinetic energy).
\item Compute connection and potential gradient.
\item Add control and external covector fields.
\item Simulate intrinsic flow with and without control.
\item Use coordinate charts only for computation and plotting.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Cross-Formalism Term Correspondence and Frame Transformations}
\subsection{Equivalent Physics Across Representations}
For the same mechanism and state, each formalism encodes the same force balance with different coordinates.
One common generalized-coordinate statement is
\begin{equation}
M(q)\ddot q + C(q,\dot q)\dot q + g(q) - J_c^T\lambda = \tau + \tau_{\mathrm{ext}}.
\end{equation}
An equivalent spatial-screw statement is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal I\dot V + \operatorname{ad}_{V}^{*}(\mathcal I V) + \mathcal W_g - \mathcal W_{\mathrm{constr}} = \mathcal W_{\mathrm{ctrl}} + \mathcal W_{\mathrm{ext}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Term-by-Term Analogies}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Inertia term.}
Euler--Lagrange: $M(q)\ddot q$.
Newton--Euler/screw: $\mathcal I\dot V$.
Operational-space: $\Lambda(x)\ddot x$.
Geometric: inertial part of $\nabla_{\dot q}\dot q$.

\item \textbf{Velocity-product term (Coriolis/centripetal).}
Euler--Lagrange: $C(q,\dot q)\dot q$.
Newton--Euler/screw: velocity-product wrench terms, e.g. $\operatorname{ad}_{V}^{*}(\mathcal I V)$.
Operational-space: $\mu(x,\dot x)$.
Geometric: connection/Christoffel contribution in $\nabla_{\dot q}\dot q$.
PoE kinematics: bias acceleration term $\dot J\dot q$.

\item \textbf{Gravity term.}
Euler--Lagrange: $g(q)$.
Newton--Euler/screw: gravity wrenches on links.
Operational-space: $p(x)$.
Geometric: $\operatorname{grad}V(q)$.

\item \textbf{Constraint reactions.}
Euler--Lagrange: $J_c^T\lambda$.
Newton--Euler/screw: constraint/contact wrench channels.
Operational-space: $F_{\mathrm{constr}}$.
Geometric: reaction covectors restricting admissible directions.

\item \textbf{Control channel.}
Euler--Lagrange: $\tau$ (or $M^{-1}\tau$ in acceleration form).
Newton--Euler/screw: applied joint/body wrenches.
Operational-space: $F_{\mathrm{ctrl}}$ mapped by Jacobians.
Geometric: $\mathcal G(q)u$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Frame Changes and Invariants}
Let $g\in SE(3)$ map body to space frame and let $\operatorname{Ad}_g$ be the adjoint map.
Twists and wrenches transform as
\begin{equation}
V_s=\operatorname{Ad}_g V_b,
\qquad
F_s=\operatorname{Ad}_g^{-T}F_b.
\end{equation}
Instantaneous power is frame-invariant:
\begin{equation}
F_s^T V_s = F_b^T V_b.
\end{equation}
So different coordinate-frame expressions of ``Coriolis'', ``centripetal'', or ``gravity'' are not different physics; they are transformed representations of the same underlying interaction channels.

\subsection{ZTCF Anchor in Each Formalism}
At fixed state, each formalism admits the same passive anchor idea:
\begin{itemize}
\item Euler--Lagrange: evaluate $\ddot q$ with $\tau=0$.
\item Newton--Euler/screw: evaluate wrench recursion with control channels removed.
\item Operational-space: map that passive acceleration to $\ddot x_{\mathrm{ZTCF}}$ via $J$ and $\dot J\dot q$.
\item Geometric: evaluate intrinsic drift field $f_{\mathrm{drift}}$.
\end{itemize}
Control then appears as a tangent increment from that shared passive baseline.

\section{Workflow Comparison}
