\section{Hidden Loads and Internal Energy Transfer Procedure}
Compact equations can hide contact reactions, friction, actuator internals, or compliance effects.
Recover them by introducing explicit channels and solving for reaction variables.

Recommended workflow:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Add explicit constraint and external-load channels.
\item Solve for hidden variables (typically $\ddot q$ and $\lambda$).
\item Map generalized reactions to local force/torque quantities.
\item Audit both global and local power flow.
\end{enumerate}

Power-channel decomposition:
\begin{equation}
P_{\mathrm{ctrl}}=\tau_{\mathrm{ctrl}}^T\dot q,
\qquad
P_{\mathrm{drift}}=(C\dot q+g)^T\dot q,
\qquad
P_{\mathrm{constr}}=(J_c^T\lambda)^T\dot q.
\end{equation}
Even when generalized constraint work is zero, local interface loads can be large.
This is especially important in whip-like motions, where distal speed amplification depends on internal transfer timing.

\section{Symmetry, Invariance, and Conservation Laws}
Symmetry underlies physical law.
The equations of motion are objective: they do not depend on the coordinate frame chosen to describe the system.
Coordinate changes alter symbolic form, not physical content.

This is why coordinate-free formulations are especially powerful:
they make representation independence explicit and help separate true physics from chart artifacts.

Conservation laws are symmetry consequences.
By Noether's principle, continuous symmetries generate invariants:
\begin{itemize}
\item translation symmetry $\Rightarrow$ linear momentum conservation,
\item rotation symmetry $\Rightarrow$ angular momentum conservation,
\item time-translation symmetry $\Rightarrow$ energy conservation.
\end{itemize}
These are structural properties of the physics, not properties of one coordinate system.
